Question-1
Design the journey of a web request: create a neat flow diagram showing what happens when a user enter a website URL on a browser. Explain DNS, browser HTTP request, web server, HTTP response and page rendering in the correct sequence.
When a user enters a website URL in a web browser, several processes take place before the webpage is displayed.
Explanation-
1) User enters URL: First, the user enters a website address in the browser, such as www.beta.com⁠�.
2) Browser: The browser understands the URL and start finding the requested website.
3) DNS: DNS converts the website name into its IP address. This helps the browser to find the correct server.
4) HTTP Request: The browser sends a request to the web server for the required webpage.
5) Web server: The web server receives the request and finds the required files or information.
6) HTTP response: The server sends the requested data back to the browser along with a response status.
7) Page Rendering: The browser processes the HTML, CSS and JavaScript and shows the webpage on the screen.
Flow Diagram:
User Enters URL → Browser → DNS lookup → IP Address of web server → HTTP request → HTTP response → Browser receives the response → Page Rendering
Question-2
Internet vs WWW- explain with a real-life example: differentiate the Internet and world wide web. Then use a real-life example such as an online university portal to explain which parts are internet infrastructure and which parts are web services.
While often used interchangeably, the internet and the world wide web are entirely different technologies.
One Difference
• The Internet: The physical infrastructure.
It is a massive global network of connected computers, cables, routers, and data centers.
• The WWW: A service that runs on top of the internet. It is a collection of multimedia pages, documents and web services linked together by hyperlinks.
Real-life Example - Online University Portal
Imagine a university website like https://uni-portal.edu⁠�. Here is how it divides into internet infrastructure and web services.
Internet Infrastructure
• The physical servers: The heavy machines sitting in the university IT department or a cloud data center holding all the student files.
• Network cables & Wi-Fi: The campus fiber-optic cables and routers that physically transmit your data packets.
• IP Address: The unique numbers assigned to your laptop and the university server so they can locate each other over the network.
Web Services
• Student Dashboard: The interactive HTML web pages where you view your profile, grades, and announcements.
• Online Assignments submission: A web tool (script) that lets you upload a PDF file directly to a professor's digital folder via your browser.
• Course Registration API: A web system that handles payment transactions securely and instantly updates the university course database when you enroll.
